2. DESIGN SYSTEM — NON-NEGOTIABLE
==================================================

Create an exceptionally polished, premium visual experience combining DARK GLASSMORPHISM with RESTRAINED NEUMORPHISM.

The interface must look like a sophisticated, modern venture-backed African technology startup.

A. Color system

Primary background: #0B1220
Secondary background: #101927
Elevated surface: #162235
Glass surface: rgba(255,255,255,0.055)
Glass border: rgba(255,255,255,0.11)
Primary accent: #B8F34A
Accent hover: #C8FF69
Primary text: #F5F7FA
Secondary text: #A6B2C2
Muted text: #78869A
Success: accessible green
Warning: accessible amber
Error: accessible red

Use lime green selectively for important calls to action, active states, key metrics, progress indicators, and meaningful highlights.

Do not turn the entire interface neon green.

B. Glassmorphism

Use translucent surfaces, layered backgrounds, subtle backdrop blur, delicate one-pixel borders, soft reflections, and restrained shadows.

Apply glass effects to:
- Main navigation and floating navigation panels.
- Dashboard summary cards.
- Search and filter surfaces.
- Project preview cards.
- Profile panels.
- Modal dialogs and popovers.
- Selected tabs and contextual action panels.

Create genuine depth between the page background, floating panels, content cards, and foreground controls.

Keep body text and important data highly readable. Never sacrifice accessibility for transparency.

C. Neumorphism

Use restrained neumorphic details for:
- Small icon containers.
- Secondary action buttons.
- Segmented controls.
- Toggle controls.
- Compact metric indicators.

Avoid excessive inset shadows, low-contrast text, and the dated appearance of fully neumorphic interfaces.

D. Background treatment

Use deep navy gradients, very subtle ambient lime illumination, optional blurred radial light fields, and low-opacity geometric texture.

Keep the background elegant and restrained.

Avoid excessive glowing borders, large distracting animations, and visual clutter.

E. Typography

Use a clean modern sans-serif typeface such as Inter or an equivalent available font.

Establish a clear hierarchy:
- Large, confident landing-page headlines.
- Compact dashboard headings.
- Readable body copy.
- Clear labels and metadata.
- Monospaced or tabular numerals where useful for metrics.

F. Components

Create reusable components for:
- Primary and secondary buttons.
- Glass cards.
- Form controls.
- Search fields.
- Skill chips.
- Status badges.
- Profile cards.
- Project cards.
- Progress indicators.
- Empty states.
- Loading skeletons.
- Confirmation dialogs.
- Notifications.
- Tables and pagination.
- Error messages.

G. Responsive design

Design mobile-first while delivering an excellent desktop experience.

Desktop:
Use a refined sidebar and content workspace for authenticated dashboards.

Mobile:
Use compact navigation, appropriately sized touch targets, stacked cards, and a simple bottom navigation or accessible mobile menu.

Avoid horizontal overflow and desktop-only interactions.

Respect reduced-motion preferences and provide accessible keyboard focus states.

