4. USER ROLES AND AUTHENTICATION
==================================================

Implement three distinct user roles.

ROLE A: TALENT

Talent can:
- Register and sign in.
- Create a professional profile.
- Add skills and experience.
- Upload or link portfolio evidence.
- Specify availability and preferred work categories.
- Discover opportunities.
- Apply to projects.
- Track applications.
- Manage active engagements.
- Submit deliverables.
- Receive feedback.
- Review completed engagements.
- Build a portfolio of verified work.

ROLE B: BUSINESS

Businesses can:
- Register and create a business profile.
- Submit appropriate verification information.
- Create and manage project listings.
- Specify budgets, deadlines, scope, deliverables, and revision limits.
- Review applications.
- Shortlist suitable candidates.
- Select talent.
- Agree on project terms.
- Review submitted work.
- Request revisions within agreed terms.
- Accept completed work.
- Leave feedback after a legitimate engagement.

ROLE C: ADMINISTRATOR

Administrators can:
- Review business verification requests.
- Moderate projects and user-generated content.
- Review reports.
- Investigate suspected fraud.
- Suspend or restrict accounts where justified.
- Manage categories and platform settings.
- Inspect platform activity and aggregate analytics.
- Review audit logs.

Implement role-aware routing and navigation.

IMPORTANT SECURITY REQUIREMENT:
Frontend role checks are not sufficient. Enforce authorization on the server or through supported database security policies.

Users must never be able to access or modify another user's private records by manipulating URLs or client-side requests.

